 "assumptions": [
  "FX: P&L in USD on a USD notional, long = long USD against the local currency, marked at the close: N x (S_t - S_0) / S_t. EUR/USD is quoted in dollars per euro, so long = long EUR and P&L = N x (S_t - S_0) / S_0.",
  "FX carry accrues daily on calendar days (actual/360) at the rate differential: long USD/BRL pays the Selic and earns US fed funds, short earns it; long EUR/USD earns the ECB deposit rate and pays fed funds. Policy rates stand in for deposit rates.",
  "Bonds, DI proxies and the OAT-Bund spread: P&L = -direction x notional x duration x change in yield. Long = long the bond (gains when the yield falls); for the spread, long = long OAT against Bund (gains when the spread narrows).",
  "Duration: 8 years for the US 10-year and the France-Germany spread, 5.5 years for Brazil's and Mexico's 10-year positions. Brazil's NTN-F and Mexico's Mbonos pay coupons near 10-13% (and yield 9-15%), so their modified duration is about 5-6 years, not the 8 of a low-coupon US or euro bond; using 8 would overstate their P&L by about 45%. DI proxies are zero-coupon: duration = years to maturity / (1 + yield), fixed at entry.",
  "No carry or roll-down on bond and spread positions.",
  "Gold and silver (XAUUSD, XAGUSD): spot in US dollars per troy ounce; long = long the metal. P&L = direction x notional x (P_t - P_0) / P_0. Metals pay no interest, so a long pays the US funding rate (Fed funds target, upper limit) on the notional, actual/360, and a short earns it.",
  "Costs are a full round trip charged at entry, so open P&L is liquidation value: FX a percentage of notional, bonds a yield amount times duration times notional.",
  "Sizing by risk: notional = risk amount / loss per unit of notional between the entry close and the stop (costs excluded)."
 ],
